window.WIKI_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "The Canopy wiki", "section": "Start here", "lede": "Guides for learning git with Canopy, recipes for everyday fixes, and a complete reference. New to git? Start with Git in ten minutes.", "headings": [{"id": "guides", "text": "Guides"}, {"id": "git-in-ten-minutes", "text": "Git in ten minutes"}, {"id": "your-daily-workflow", "text": "Your daily workflow"}, {"id": "branches-merging-and-rebasing", "text": "Branches, merging and rebasing"}, {"id": "working-with-github", "text": "Working with GitHub"}, {"id": "recipes", "text": "Recipes"}, {"id": "fix-your-last-commit", "text": "Fix your last commit"}, {"id": "undo-a-mistake", "text": "Undo a mistake"}, {"id": "resolve-a-merge-conflict", "text": "Resolve a merge conflict"}, {"id": "tidy-up-commits-before-sharing", "text": "Tidy up commits before sharing"}, {"id": "find-the-commit-that-broke-something", "text": "Find the commit that broke something"}, {"id": "work-on-two-branches-at-once", "text": "Work on two branches at once"}, {"id": "review-a-pull-request", "text": "Review a pull request"}, {"id": "reference", "text": "Reference"}, {"id": "configuration", "text": "Configuration"}, {"id": "command-line", "text": "Command line"}, {"id": "themes", "text": "Themes"}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting"}, {"id": "faq", "text": "FAQ"}], "text": "New here? Read Git in ten minutes , then Your daily workflow . Already comfortable with git? Jump to the recipes, or look up a key on the Keys page. Guides  Git in ten minutes  Repositories, the staging area, commits, branches and remotes, each shown where it lives in Canopy. Your daily workflow  Edit, stage, commit, push, open a PR: the loop you'll do every day. Branches, merging and rebasing  Why branches, how merging and rebasing differ, and what to do when they collide. Working with GitHub  Connect GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI from your terminal. Recipes  Fix your last commit  Typo in the message, forgot a file, or want to fold a fix into an older commit. Undo a mistake  Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back \"lost\" work. Resolve a merge conflict  Resolve conflicts one at a time, then finish the merge or rebase. Tidy up commits before sharing  Squash, reorder, reword or drop commits with a visual interactive rebase. Find the commit that broke something  Bisect to the breaking commit, then blame and file history to understand it. Work on two branches at once  Check out a second branch in its own folder with worktrees. Review a pull request  Check out a PR, read the diff and checks, then approve or request changes. Reference  Configuration  Every setting in config.toml, with its default. Command line  Flags, arguments and environment variables. Themes  The five built-in themes and their colors. Troubleshooting  Fixes for the problems people actually hit. FAQ  Short answers to common questions."}, {"url": "git-basics.html", "title": "Git in ten minutes", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The handful of ideas that make git make sense, and where to see each one in Canopy.", "headings": [{"id": "repo", "text": "A repository is a project with a memory"}, {"id": "three-places", "text": "Your changes live in three places"}, {"id": "commits", "text": "A commit is a saved snapshot"}, {"id": "branches", "text": "A branch is a line of work"}, {"id": "remotes", "text": "A remote is the same repo somewhere else"}, {"id": "safety", "text": "You can almost always undo"}], "text": "A repository is a project with a memory  A repository (repo) is a folder whose history git tracks. Everything git knows lives in a hidden .git folder inside it. Run canopy in any repo and the Home tab ( 1 ) shows where things stand. canopy — Home Your changes live in three places  The working tree is the files you edit. Changes here are listed under Changes on the Changes tab ( 2 ). The staging area (also called the index) is where you gather what goes into the next commit. Staged files appear under Staged . History is the commits themselves, on the History tab ( 3 ). Staging is what lets you commit part of your work. In Canopy you can stage a whole file with space , or step into its diff with ↵ and stage single lines. canopy — Changes A commit is a saved snapshot  A commit records the staged changes with a message saying why. Each commit points to the one before it, which is how history forms a chain. Press c , write a short summary (Canopy counts to 50 characters for you), and press ↵ . Good messages say why : \"Fix crash when the repo has no commits\" beats \"update main.rs\". A branch is a line of work  A branch is a movable name for a commit. When you commit, your current branch moves forward. Branches let you try something without disturbing main . The Branches tab ( 4 ) lists them; n makes a new one and space switches to it. HEAD means \"where you are now\"; History shows it as HEAD → main . A remote is the same repo somewhere else  A remote (usually called origin ) is a copy of the repo on a server such as GitHub. Push ( P ) sends your commits there; pull ( p ) brings others' commits to you; fetch ( f ) checks what's new without changing your files. The header shows ↑2 ↓1 when you're 2 commits ahead and 1 behind. You can almost always undo  Git rarely forgets anything. z undoes your last commit, reset, merge or checkout, and the Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere you've been so you can go back further. See Undo a mistake . Learn as you go: keep teach mode on (it is by default). The footer shows the git command behind every action you take, so the ideas on this page turn into commands you'll recognize."}, {"url": "daily-workflow.html", "title": "Your daily workflow", "section": "Guides", "lede": "The loop you'll repeat every day: pull, branch, edit, stage, commit, push, open a pull request.", "headings": [{"id": "tips", "text": "Tips"}], "text": "Start up to date. Open Canopy in your repo and press p to pull. Home tells you if you're behind. Make a branch for your change. Go to Branches ( 4 ), press n , and name it after the change, like fix-login-timeout . Canopy switches to it. Edit your files in your editor as usual. Canopy notices changes within a few seconds; ⌃R refreshes right away. Review and stage. On Changes ( 2 ), read each file's diff on the right. Press space to stage a file, or ↵ to go into the diff and stage only some lines. Commit. Press c , write a summary, press ↵ . Repeat steps 3–5 as you go: small commits are easier to review and to undo. Push. Press P . The first push of a new branch also sets it up to track the remote. Open a pull request. Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press n . Give it a title and a description of what changed and why. Watch CI. Actions ( 0 ) shows your branch's runs updating live. Home's GitHub card also shows whether checks pass. Tips  Not ready to commit but need to switch tasks? S stashes your changes; pop them back from the Stash tab ( 5 ). Committed too early? On Changes, A amends the last commit. See Fix your last commit . Forgot where you were? Home's Next steps says what to do next."}, {"url": "branches.html", "title": "Branches, merging and rebasing", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Why branches matter, the two ways to combine them, and what to do when they collide.", "headings": [{"id": "why", "text": "Why branch"}, {"id": "merge", "text": "Merging"}, {"id": "rebase", "text": "Rebasing"}, {"id": "conflicts", "text": "When they collide"}, {"id": "tracking", "text": "Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\""}], "text": "Why branch  A branch keeps a change separate until it's ready. main stays working while you experiment, and you can drop a branch without affecting anything else. Branches are cheap: make one for every change. Merging  Merging brings another branch's commits into yours. If both branches moved on, git makes a merge commit with two parents; History draws it as ○ where two lines join. On Branches ( 4 ), select the branch to bring in and press M . Rebasing  Rebasing replays your commits on top of another branch, as if you'd started from its latest commit. History stays a straight line, with no merge commit. Select the branch to rebase onto and press r . Which should I use? Merge when a branch's history matters as its own story, or when others already have your commits. Rebase to keep your own unshared work tidy before opening a PR. Never rebase commits other people have pulled: rebasing rewrites them. When they collide  If both branches changed the same lines, git stops and asks you to choose. The header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE ), and Home says what to do. Follow Resolve a merge conflict . Upstreams and \"ahead / behind\"  A branch's upstream is the remote branch it syncs with, like origin/main . The Branches tab shows ↑1 ↓2 (1 commit to push, 2 to pull) or ✓ when in sync. u sets the upstream if it's missing; pushing a new branch with P sets it for you."}, {"url": "github-flow.html", "title": "Working with GitHub", "section": "Guides", "lede": "Connect Canopy to GitHub, then handle pull requests, issues and CI without leaving the terminal.", "headings": [{"id": "connect", "text": "Connect GitHub"}, {"id": "prs", "text": "Pull requests (8)"}, {"id": "issues", "text": "Issues (9)"}, {"id": "actions", "text": "Actions (0)"}], "text": "Connect GitHub  Canopy uses the official GitHub CLI, gh , with your own login. Install and log in once: $ brew install gh $ gh auth login Canopy never installs anything for you. If gh is missing or logged out, the GitHub tabs say exactly what to run. Pull requests (8)  See every open PR with its checks and review state. f cycles open, yours, review requested and all. The side panel shows the description, checks, reviews and comments; D adds the diff. canopy — Pull requests Issues (9)  Browse issues with labels and comments. n files a new one, C comments, X closes (or reopens). Actions (0)  Runs for your branch, updating live. Select a failed run to see the failing step and the end of its log, then R to re-run it. For the full key list, see the GitHub page ."}, {"url": "fix-last-commit.html", "title": "Fix your last commit", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "A typo in the message, a forgotten file, or a fix that belongs in an older commit.", "headings": [{"id": "message", "text": "Change the last commit's message"}, {"id": "forgot", "text": "Add a forgotten file"}, {"id": "older", "text": "Fold a fix into an older commit"}], "text": "Change the last commit's message  On Changes ( 2 ), press A (amend). The dialog opens with the current message. Edit it and press ↵ . Add a forgotten file  On Changes ( 2 ), stage the file with space . Press A , keep the message, press ↵ . The file is now part of the last commit. Fold a fix into an older commit  Stage the fix. On History ( 3 ), select the commit it belongs to and press F . Canopy makes a fixup! commit. To squash it in, press i on the commit before it and start the rebase; see Tidy up commits . Already pushed? Amending rewrites the commit, so the next push needs force. Canopy offers \"force push (with lease)\", which refuses if someone else pushed in the meantime. Only do this on branches nobody else is using."}, {"url": "undo.html", "title": "Undo a mistake", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Undo a commit, a reset, a merge or a checkout, and get back work you thought was lost.", "headings": [{"id": "z", "text": "The quick way: z"}, {"id": "further", "text": "Going further back: the reflog"}, {"id": "discard", "text": "Throw away uncommitted edits"}], "text": "The quick way: z  z undoes the last thing that moved your branch, and tells you exactly what it will do before it does it: After a commit: the commit is undone and its changes come back staged , ready to fix and commit again. After a checkout: you switch back to where you were. After a reset, merge or rebase: the branch moves back and your uncommitted changes are kept. Going further back: the reflog  The Reflog tab ( 7 ) lists everywhere HEAD has been, including commits no branch points to any more. To get back to any of them: Open Reflog ( 7 ) and find the entry from before the mistake. The side panel shows that commit. Press r and choose Mixed to move your branch there and keep your files as they are, or Hard to make your files match it too. Throw away uncommitted edits  On Changes, select the file and press d . Canopy tells you exactly what will be lost and asks first. This one can't be undone, because git never saved those edits."}, {"url": "conflicts.html", "title": "Resolve a merge conflict", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "When a merge, rebase or cherry-pick stops because both sides changed the same lines.", "headings": [], "text": "You'll know you have a conflict because the header shows MERGE IN PROGRESS (or REBASE or CHERRY-PICK ) and Home says how many files are conflicted. Go to Changes ( 2 ). Conflicted files are listed first, under Conflicts . Select one and press ↵ . The panel shows each conflict with ours (your side) and theirs (the side you're bringing in). For each conflict, press o to keep yours, t to take theirs, or b to keep both. j / k move between conflicts. Canopy writes the file as you go. When a file has no conflicts left, Canopy stages it for you. When all files are resolved, press C to continue the merge or rebase. canopy — Conflicts Need something in between? Press e to open the file in your editor, fix it by hand, save, and come back. Changed your mind about a choice? u puts the conflict markers back. Want out entirely? X aborts and returns everything to how it was."}, {"url": "clean-history.html", "title": "Tidy up commits before sharing", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Squash, reorder and drop commits with a visual interactive rebase.", "headings": [], "text": "Before opening a PR, you might want to turn \"wip\", \"fix typo\" and \"actually fix it\" into one clear commit. That's an interactive rebase . On History ( 3 ), select the oldest commit you want to change and press i . The rebase editor lists those commits, oldest at the top. For each one choose: p pick : keep it as is. s squash : combine with the commit above, keeping both messages. f fixup : combine with the commit above, dropping this message. e edit : stop at this commit so you can change it. d drop : remove it. Move commits up and down with K and J . Press ↵ to run it. If it stops on a conflict, resolve it as in Resolve a merge conflict and press C . Don't rewrite shared history. Only tidy commits you haven't pushed, or ones on a branch that's only yours. If the rebase goes wrong, z undoes it."}, {"url": "find-a-bug.html", "title": "Find the commit that broke something", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Bisect to the commit that introduced a bug, then use blame and file history to understand it.", "headings": [{"id": "bisect", "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit"}, {"id": "blame", "text": "Blame: who changed this line, and why"}, {"id": "history", "text": "File history"}], "text": "Bisect: find the breaking commit  Commit or stash your changes (bisect checks out other commits). On History ( 3 ), select a commit where things still worked and press b . Your current commit counts as broken. Git checks out a commit halfway between. Test it: build it, run it, whatever shows the bug. Press b and answer good or bad (or skip if you can't test it). The header shows about how many steps are left. When git finds the culprit, choose Finish and show it . Canopy stops bisecting and selects that commit in History. Blame: who changed this line, and why  On Changes, or on a file inside a commit's diff, press B . Each line shows the commit that last changed it. ↵ jumps to that commit; B again shows the file as it was just before that change, so you can keep digging. canopy — Blame File history  Press L on a file to see only the commits that touched it, following renames. esc goes back to the full history."}, {"url": "two-branches.html", "title": "Work on two branches at once", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Keep a second branch checked out in its own folder with worktrees.", "headings": [], "text": "Switching branches means stashing or committing half-done work. A worktree avoids that: a second folder for the same repository, with a different branch checked out. Go to Branches ( 4 ) and press ] until you reach Worktrees . Press n and type a branch name (existing or new). Canopy creates the folder next to your repo: acme-app + fix/login becomes acme-app-fix-login . Press ↵ to open it in Canopy, or open the folder in your editor. When you're done, select it and press d to remove it. If you deleted the folder by hand, x tidies up."}, {"url": "review-a-pr.html", "title": "Review a pull request", "section": "Recipes", "lede": "Check out a PR, read it, run it, and leave a review.", "headings": [], "text": "Go to Pull requests ( 8 ) and press f until it shows review requested . Select the PR. The side panel has the description, checks and existing reviews. Press D to read the diff. Want to run it? Press space to check out its branch. Press r and choose Approve , Comment , or Request changes . Write your review (Markdown works) and press ⌃S . If it's approved and checks pass, M merges it: merge commit, squash, or rebase. canopy — Pull requests"}, {"url": "config.html", "title": "Configuration", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Every setting in config.toml, with its default.", "headings": [{"id": "keys", "text": "Remapping keys"}, {"id": "custom", "text": "Custom commands"}], "text": "The config file is ~/.config/canopy/config.toml (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopy/config.toml ). canopy --config-path prints where Canopy looks; canopy --example-config prints a starter file. Every setting is optional. theme \"canopy\" Color theme: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord or light . See Themes . teach_mode true Show the git command behind each action in the footer. T toggles it while running. confirm_destructive true Ask before discarding changes, hard resets, force deletes and similar. compact false Tighter layout: less padding and no gaps between panels. splash true Play the tree animation when Canopy starts (any key skips it). mac_key_symbols macOS: true , else false Show modifier keys as ⌃ ⌥ ⇧ instead of ctrl- / alt- . nerd_font false Use Nerd Font icons (only turn on if your terminal font has them). workspace_dirs [] Folders the Workspace tab scans for repositories, e.g. [\"~/code\"] . Empty means the folder above the current repo. workspace_depth 3 How many folders deep to look for repositories. log_page_size 300 Commits History loads at a time (more load as you scroll). gh_program unset Path to the GitHub CLI if it isn't gh on your PATH . [keys] empty Remap actions. See below. [[custom_commands]] none Run your own commands from a key. See below. Remapping keys  Map an action's name to a key or a list of keys. Names are in the last column of the Keys page. A key you assign is taken away from whatever used it before, and Canopy warns you if that leaves an action without a key. [keys] toggle_stage = \"s\" commit = [\"c\", \"ctrl-s\"] push = \"alt-p\" Key names: a character ( x , X , ? ), space , enter , esc , tab , backtab , arrows ( up …), pageup , home , f1 – f12 , with ctrl- and/or alt- in front. Custom commands  [[custom_commands]] key = \"X\" cmd = \"git push --force-with-lease\" description = \"force push (safely)\" confirm = true The command runs with sh -c in the repository's root. Its output is shown when it finishes. description is what the command palette and help show; confirm asks first."}, {"url": "cli.html", "title": "Command line", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Flags, arguments and environment variables.", "headings": [{"id": "env", "text": "Environment variables"}], "text": "$ canopy [OPTIONS] [PATH] PATH Repository to open. Defaults to the current folder. -w , --workspace DIR Start in the Workspace tab, scanning DIR for repositories. -t , --theme THEME Use a theme for this run: canopy , catppuccin , gruvbox , nord , light . --config-path Print where Canopy reads its config, then exit. --example-config Print a starter config, then exit. -V , --version Print the version. -h , --help Print help. Environment variables  CANOPY_CONFIG Read the config from this file instead of the default location. XDG_CONFIG_HOME If set, the config lives at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/canopy/config.toml . CANOPY_GH Path to the GitHub CLI (same as gh_program in the config). VISUAL , EDITOR The editor e opens files in (falls back to vi )."}, {"url": "themes.html", "title": "Themes", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Five built-in themes. Set one with theme = \"…\" in the config, pass -t, or cycle live with ⌃T.", "headings": [], "text": "Themes use 24-bit color. If colors look wrong, your terminal may not support it; see Troubleshooting ."}, {"url": "troubleshooting.html", "title": "Troubleshooting", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Fixes for the problems people actually run into.", "headings": [{"id": "not-a-repo", "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\""}, {"id": "gh", "text": "GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in"}, {"id": "option", "text": "Option shortcuts don't work"}, {"id": "glyphs", "text": "Borders or symbols look misaligned"}, {"id": "colors", "text": "Colors look wrong or washed out"}, {"id": "push", "text": "Push was rejected"}, {"id": "config", "text": "\"Config error, using defaults\""}, {"id": "bug", "text": "Something else"}], "text": "\"is not inside a git repository\"  You passed a folder that isn't a repo. Run canopy inside a repository, or use canopy -w ~/code to pick one from the Workspace tab. GitHub tabs say gh isn't installed or logged in  Install the GitHub CLI and log in, then reopen Canopy: brew install gh , gh auth login . If gh lives somewhere unusual, set gh_program in the config. Option shortcuts don't work  Canopy understands both terminals that send Option as Alt/Meta and macOS Terminal's default, where Option types a special character on a US keyboard layout. With other keyboard layouts, turn on Use Option as Meta key (Terminal → Settings → Profiles → Keyboard) or the equivalent in iTerm2 (Profiles → Keys → Left Option key: Esc+). Borders or symbols look misaligned  Canopy only draws characters that are one column wide in standard monospace fonts. If lines still don't meet, your terminal font may be missing box-drawing characters: try SF Mono, Menlo, JetBrains Mono or Fira Code. In iTerm2, turn off Treat ambiguous-width characters as double width . Colors look wrong or washed out  Canopy uses 24-bit (\"true\") color. Some terminals, including older versions of macOS Terminal, don't support it and approximate the colors; iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and Alacritty do support it. Trying another theme with ⌃T can help too. Push was rejected  Someone pushed to the branch before you. Press p to pull first; if your branch has diverged, P offers to pull with rebase, pull with merge, or force push with lease. \"Config error, using defaults\"  The config file has a typo. The message names the line. canopy --example-config prints a valid starting point. Something else  Open an issue with what you did, what you expected, and your terminal and OS."}, {"url": "faq.html", "title": "FAQ", "section": "Reference", "lede": "Short answers to common questions.", "headings": [{"id": "does-canopy-replace-git", "text": "Does Canopy replace git?"}, {"id": "is-it-safe-to-use-on-important-repositories", "text": "Is it safe to use on important repositories?"}, {"id": "does-it-send-my-code-anywhere", "text": "Does it send my code anywhere?"}, {"id": "does-it-work-on-linux-windows", "text": "Does it work on Linux? Windows?"}, {"id": "can-i-use-it-with-gitlab-or-bitbucket", "text": "Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?"}, {"id": "why-and-but-no-shortcuts", "text": "Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?"}, {"id": "how-do-i-update", "text": "How do I update?"}], "text": "Does Canopy replace git?  No. Canopy runs the real git for everything, so your hooks, config, signing and credential helpers all work, and you can mix it freely with the command line. Is it safe to use on important repositories?  Canopy asks before anything destructive, and z undoes most actions. Everything it does is a normal git command; turn on teach mode to see each one. Does it send my code anywhere?  No. Git runs locally. GitHub features use your own gh login and only contact GitHub when you open a GitHub tab or act on one. Does it work on Linux? Windows?  macOS and Linux, yes. Windows isn't supported yet. Can I use it with GitLab or Bitbucket?  All the git features work with any remote. The GitHub tabs are GitHub-only for now. Why ⌃ and ⌥ but no ⌘ shortcuts?  Terminals keep the Command key for themselves (copy, paste, new tab) and don't pass it to programs, so Canopy uses Control and Option. How do I update?  brew upgrade canopy , or re-run the cargo install command with --force ."}];
